1
00:00:03,370 --> 00:00:06,430
Kakushi's bridge promises that if a Maker doesn't pay,

2
00:00:06,470 --> 00:00:07,768
you can prove it.

3
00:00:07,851 --> 00:00:11,375
Proving that something did not happen needs complete,

4
00:00:11,415 --> 00:00:14,653
gap-free windows of another chain's history.

5
00:00:14,794 --> 00:00:18,995
That is the job of our Chainlink CRE workflow.

6
00:00:21,737 --> 00:00:23,450
This is the real workflow,

7
00:00:23,490 --> 00:00:28,036
run by the CRE CLI simulator against live testnet,

8
00:00:28,076 --> 00:00:31,019
read-only. A cron trigger fires,

9
00:00:31,059 --> 00:00:33,249
the workflow reads the Monad hub,

10
00:00:33,289 --> 00:00:35,977
finds the next contiguous Sepolia range,

11
00:00:36,017 --> 00:00:39,092
and pulls the Maker payouts with filterLogs.

12
00:00:40,202 --> 00:00:43,157
It builds sorted Poseidon2 roots for that window,

13
00:00:43,197 --> 00:00:46,872
and writeReport sends them to the AttestationOracle on

14
00:00:46,912 --> 00:00:51,215
Monad. This run computed the Sepolia window right after the

15
00:00:51,255 --> 00:00:53,226
one that holds our bridge payout.

16
00:00:53,702 --> 00:00:57,133
On Monad, the hosted runner delivers those reports.

17
00:00:57,173 --> 00:00:59,467
This transaction, from the runner's key,

18
00:00:59,507 --> 00:01:01,595
lands windows on the oracle.

19
00:01:02,225 --> 00:01:05,215
To be clear: the on-chain windows come from Kakushi's

20
00:01:05,255 --> 00:01:07,607
runner, running the same logic through Chainlink's

21
00:01:07,647 --> 00:01:09,270
MockKeystoneForwarder.

22
00:01:09,481 --> 00:01:12,591
It checks no signatures, so this is not a DON yet.

23
00:01:13,037 --> 00:01:14,834
The app shows every window.

24
00:01:14,976 --> 00:01:16,758
When a Maker misses a deadline,

25
00:01:16,798 --> 00:01:19,661
the dispute circuit proves the missing payout against these

26
00:01:19,701 --> 00:01:23,034
roots, and the Maker's margin pays the user.

